527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1031 "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1033 3029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050527 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186175591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175588 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "714d9cd6-ccc3-57b3-a248-3dddcfab66af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175591 #edb/ref 1080 "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175591 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175591 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ff731292-d0a7-5a02-8229-24bf6fdc3022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175780 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175780 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of \n![](images/cc/cc6a84d8a040240089d946999190e4d118fee2be70df2958a6a59d6534bcf429.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175780 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175780 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/1c/1c7fa984e0d00619ea2e8647f560efc3f928b643875f0cbf973ca8bb2a931e3e.svg)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](images/ec/ec3f75c25f412d466b7acfc4810ecf6987038a23ee5da0dfdd98cc181e37e0c7.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175781 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175781 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine whether ![](images/85/8594aa29c1348769c738a5ed407df28d27324d8e6d8679971b3a5825de6aac68.svg) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](images/81/8111e3afdd5628fe312f17d6632b2d8c5204ddc9e7141e2e167ea560aad55e83.svg)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175781 #edb/ref 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175782 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175782 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ![](images/53/5337ed67f212c0029ae2317c658672aa3f3f68a7614c8ce4dfafb6e92d2f2229.svg) and ![](images/4f/4fad8749e9d06a4cafdd089e6ed982131a8957fff201d865fb9da323c5c27bf5.svg) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](images/a0/a05c08cb757f62c9b874fce3b3ae9db1d4278f1f96194a3e92df0a4d457b591c.svg)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175782 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175782 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/33/334bab555d470ebcc0d36eb4e2140530d4f92b2fa13e4adbfea848250d92de7b.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](images/82/82d57d12bd8027fc3c7e9b368b752616ed33ffcce577b956c91cb8a721dd5905.svg) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](images/8c/8cd66a9324ee5cf95277457fc1eef8ddb30d36543b71d4446eb93b5a2d8313a7.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921861759