\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-49-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}49{\\text{i}}^{2}-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=7\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7\\text{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326746 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326746 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326746 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326746 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326746 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4febda2-345b-5458-b675-3b62332f742d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326746 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6+3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -15 & -6+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(6+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+(-6+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{\\text{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348487 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348487 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348487 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0489168e-93f4-5458-8a02-7bdcd1fa6253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348525 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348525 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348525 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09a2284a-eb43-562f-941d-87d7220072bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348912 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33f237e1-cab4-5534-9c5e-564a219d644a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348936 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}