ned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375433 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "16922444-299f-55a9-a2be-066c9885244c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375434 #edb/ref 1080 "-2\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375434 #edb/ref 